steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1793183241
      nanos: 689805604
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "bd98b8f6-1eba-4968-a085-76c958373f68"
      step_index: 1
      cascade_id: "bd98b8f6-1eba-4968-a085-76c958373f68"
    }
    model_api_content_id: "f2d0b6de3de44be7abcba382d78b60e4"
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Build a complete native Android application called **Touchscreen Test Tool** that helps users inspect touch coverage, identify potentially unresponsive areas, and test simultaneous multitouch input.\n\n## 1. Platform and requirements\n\n- Use Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, and Material 3.\n- Deliver an Android Studio project with complete source code, Gradle configuration, manifest, resources, and build instructions.\n- Build a native Android app, not a website, WebView, or browser simulation.\n- If your environment cannot build or preview Android projects, clearly state that limitation and still provide the native project files. Do not silently substitute a web app.\n- The app must work entirely offline.\n- No accounts, backend, cloud storage, paid APIs, advertisements, subscriptions, analytics, or unnecessary permissions.\n- Do not request internet permission.\n- Use Android platform APIs and freely available dependencies. Explain any dependency licenses or material limitations.\n- No placeholder buttons, fake readings, simulated touch data, or unfinished TODOs.\n\n## 2. Visual design\n\nCreate a polished utility app with:\n\n- Dark navy background, bright cyan accents, and green coverage indicators.\n- Large readable text, rounded cards, clean spacing, and clear icons.\n- Light and dark themes, with dark theme as the default.\n- Accessible contrast and controls with comfortable touch targets.\n- Responsive layouts for different screen sizes and orientations.\n- Minimal visual clutter during tests.\n\nHome screen:\n\n- App name and short description.\n- Main actions: \342\200\234Touch Coverage Test\342\200\235 and \342\200\234Multitouch Test.\342\200\235\n- Secondary actions: \342\200\234How to Test,\342\200\235 \342\200\234Settings,\342\200\235 and \342\200\234About.\342\200\235\n- A short explanation: \342\200\234Check where your screen registers touch and how many fingers it detects at once.\342\200\235\n- A visible limitation: \342\200\234This app shows touch events received by Android. It cannot repair your screen or confirm a hardware fault.\342\200\235\n\n## 3. Three-second splash screen\n\nImplement a branded splash experience lasting approximately **3 seconds** on a fresh app launch.\n\nShow:\n\n- A simple touchscreen or fingertip icon.\n- \342\200\234Touchscreen Test Tool.\342\200\235\n- Tagline: \342\200\234Check every touch.\342\200\235\n- A subtle loading animation.\n\nImplementation requirements:\n\n- Integrate correctly with Android\342\200\231s system splash behavior.\n- Avoid two consecutive branded splash screens or adding an extra 3-second delay after a separate custom splash.\n- Count the intentional splash duration from the beginning of the app\342\200\231s launch presentation as closely as the platform allows.\n- Do not block the UI thread or use Thread.sleep.\n- Transition automatically to Home.\n- Do not replay the splash when rotating the device, returning from a test, or briefly resuming the app.\n- Explain that system startup or a busy device can make total launch time exceed exactly 3 seconds.\n\n## 4. Touch Coverage Test\n\nCreate a nearly full-screen test canvas with a visible grid.\n\n### Interaction\n\n- Users drag one or more fingers across the screen.\n- Cells change color when an actual touch path passes through them.\n- Show an immediate touch marker and optional trail.\n- Preserve covered cells until Reset or the session ends.\n- Use clear states:\n  - Untested: dark gray.\n  - Touched: green.\n  - Active touch: cyan.\n- Include concise instructions: \342\200\234Drag across every cell. Gray cells have not registered a touch during this test.\342\200\235\n\n### Coverage accuracy\n\n- Define coverage as the percentage of grid cells visited by recorded touch paths.\n- Display touched cells, total cells, and coverage percentage.\n- Map coordinates to the actual drawable test bounds, accounting for insets and layout.\n- Process historical touch samples when available.\n- Interpolate between successive samples from the same active pointer to avoid gaps caused by event spacing.\n- Do not connect separate gestures or different fingers.\n- Clip paths to canvas bounds.\n- Explain in How to Test that interpolation estimates the path between reported samples; a filled cell is not a guarantee that every pixel in it is responsive.\n- Never automatically label an untouched cell \342\200\234dead\342\200\235 or \342\200\234broken.\342\200\235 Use \342\200\234Untested\342\200\235 or \342\200\234Potential issue\342\200\224retest this area.\342\200\235\n\n### Controls\n\nProvide:\n\n- Reset, with confirmation if progress exists.\n- Grid density: coarse, medium, and fine.\n- Show/hide trails.\n- Show/hide grid.\n- Finish Test.\n- Elapsed time and coverage percentage.\n\nWhen grid density changes, clearly explain that coverage will reset and request confirmation if necessary.\n\n### Results\n\nShow:\n\n- Coverage percentage.\n- Touched and untouched cell counts.\n- Test duration.\n- A visual coverage map.\n- Interpretation: \342\200\234Untouched areas may have been missed. Repeat the test carefully before drawing conclusions.\342\200\235\n- Retest and Return Home actions.\n\nDo not declare the hardware healthy solely because coverage reaches 100%.\n\n## 5. Multitouch Test\n\nCreate a full-screen multitouch canvas.\n\nFor each active finger:\n\n- Draw a clearly visible colored circle.\n- Use a stable color based on pointer ID.\n- Label the pointer and display its X/Y coordinates within the test canvas.\n- Optionally draw a short fading trail.\n\nDisplay:\n\n- Current simultaneous touches.\n- Peak simultaneous touches observed during this session.\n- Session duration.\n- A clear Reset Peak action.\n\n### Correct pointer handling\n\nUse real Android MotionEvent data through an appropriate native View/Compose integration or an equally reliable approach.\n\nCorrectly handle:\n\n- ACTION_DOWN.\n- ACTION_POINTER_DOWN.\n- ACTION_MOVE.\n- ACTION_POINTER_UP.\n- ACTION_UP.\n- ACTION_CANCEL.\n- Stable pointer IDs versus changing pointer indices.\n- Multiple fingers lifting or moving independently.\n- Cancellation, app backgrounding, and interruption.\n- Clearing active contacts without erasing the recorded session peak.\n\nNever hardcode a claimed maximum supported touch count.\n\nUse wording such as:\n\n\342\200\234Peak observed: 5 simultaneous touches.\342\200\235\n\nExplain that the peak observed during a session is not proof of the device\342\200\231s absolute hardware limit.\n\nDo not fabricate pressure, touch size, sampling rate, or latency measurements. These are not required for the core app.\n\n## 6. Full-screen behavior and edge testing\n\n- Support an immersive test mode to maximize available test space.\n- Keep the screen awake only while a test is active.\n- Restore normal system UI behavior after leaving a test.\n- Provide a visible, reliable Exit control.\n- Let the control panel collapse so users can test the space beneath it.\n- Exclude areas covered by active controls from coverage calculations or clearly show that they are unavailable.\n- Explain that system gestures, navigation areas, display cutouts, and OS-reserved regions may limit edge testing.\n- Do not claim the app can capture touches that Android does not deliver.\n\nEnsure users can exit even when part of their screen is difficult to use. Preserve normal system Back behavior where permitted by Android.\n\n## 7. Settings\n\nInclude only functional settings:\n\n- Theme: dark, light, or system.\n- Grid density.\n- Touch trail visibility.\n- Coordinate visibility in multitouch mode.\n- Keep screen awake during testing.\n\nSave lightweight preferences locally using DataStore.\n\nDo not add a database unless a real requirement needs one.\n\n## 8. Lifecycle and reliability\n\n- Keep test state in an appropriate ViewModel.\n- Preserve progress across ordinary configuration changes.\n- Clear active pointers when the app loses focus or a gesture is canceled.\n- Pause elapsed active-test time while the app is backgrounded.\n- Avoid accidentally marking cells when users tap test controls.\n- Handle orientation changes with an explicit policy that prevents misleading coverage: either maintain a valid normalized mapping or reset coverage with a clear explanation.\n- Document whether unfinished sessions survive process death. Do not imply persistence that has not been implemented.\n\n## 9. Help and diagnostic limits\n\nProvide a short How to Test screen:\n\n1. Clean and dry the screen.\n2. Start Touch Coverage Test.\n3. Slowly drag across all grid cells.\n4. Repeat around any area that consistently fails to respond.\n5. Open Multitouch Test and place several fingers on the screen.\n6. Observe the current and peak touch counts.\n\nClearly explain:\n\n- Untouched cells may simply have been missed.\n- Screen protectors, moisture, gloves, and system gestures can affect results.\n- The app cannot calibrate, repair, or definitively diagnose touchscreen hardware.\n- No software test can observe a touch event the operating system never reports.\n- Multitouch results depend on the contacts actually detected during the session.\n\nUse cautious, helpful wording without alarming users.\n\n## 10. Performance and accessibility\n\n- Keep touch rendering responsive.\n- Avoid expensive allocations and full-screen recompositions on every event.\n- Bound trail history so memory usage does not grow indefinitely.\n- Use native Canvas drawing where appropriate.\n- Provide content descriptions for standard controls.\n- Do not rely solely on color for instructions or results.\n- Support readable text scaling.\n- Explain any limitations when accessibility services consume or transform touch gestures.\n\n## 11. Verification\n\nInclude focused tests or a concrete manual verification checklist covering:\n\n- The splash appears once and transitions after approximately 3 seconds.\n- Touch coordinates align with the displayed grid.\n- Slow and fast swipes produce sensible coverage.\n- Separate gestures are never connected.\n- Multiple pointers retain stable identities.\n- Lifting one finger does not remove unrelated contacts.\n- Active count returns to zero after release or cancellation.\n- Peak count remains until reset.\n- Reset and grid changes behave correctly.\n- Backgrounding and rotation do not produce false coverage.\n- Test controls do not add coverage.\n- Screen-awake behavior stops after leaving the test.\n- The app runs without a network connection.\n\nDo not claim that a build or test passed unless you actually ran it. Distinguish completed checks from checks requiring a physical Android device.\n\n## 12. Deliverables\n\nProvide:\n\n1. A short implementation overview.\n2. The complete project structure and all required source files.\n3. Exact Android Studio setup and run instructions.\n4. A brief explanation of the touch-event processing and coverage calculation.\n5. Verification results and remaining physical-device checks.\n6. Any limitations stated plainly.\n\nPrioritize accurate touch handling, a usable test canvas, and a polished 3-second splash experience. Implement the complete core app before adding optional features.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Build an Android app\"\nicon: \"android_color\"\n---\n\n"
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      text: "Build a complete native Android application called **Touchscreen Test Tool** that helps users inspect touch coverage, identify potentially unresponsive areas, and test simultaneous multitouch input.\n\n## 1. Platform and requirements\n\n- Use Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, and Material 3.\n- Deliver an Android Studio project with complete source code, Gradle configuration, manifest, resources, and build instructions.\n- Build a native Android app, not a website, WebView, or browser simulation.\n- If your environment cannot build or preview Android projects, clearly state that limitation and still provide the native project files. Do not silently substitute a web app.\n- The app must work entirely offline.\n- No accounts, backend, cloud storage, paid APIs, advertisements, subscriptions, analytics, or unnecessary permissions.\n- Do not request internet permission.\n- Use Android platform APIs and freely available dependencies. Explain any dependency licenses or material limitations.\n- No placeholder buttons, fake readings, simulated touch data, or unfinished TODOs.\n\n## 2. Visual design\n\nCreate a polished utility app with:\n\n- Dark navy background, bright cyan accents, and green coverage indicators.\n- Large readable text, rounded cards, clean spacing, and clear icons.\n- Light and dark themes, with dark theme as the default.\n- Accessible contrast and controls with comfortable touch targets.\n- Responsive layouts for different screen sizes and orientations.\n- Minimal visual clutter during tests.\n\nHome screen:\n\n- App name and short description.\n- Main actions: \342\200\234Touch Coverage Test\342\200\235 and \342\200\234Multitouch Test.\342\200\235\n- Secondary actions: \342\200\234How to Test,\342\200\235 \342\200\234Settings,\342\200\235 and \342\200\234About.\342\200\235\n- A short explanation: \342\200\234Check where your screen registers touch and how many fingers it detects at once.\342\200\235\n- A visible limitation: \342\200\234This app shows touch events received by Android. It cannot repair your screen or confirm a hardware fault.\342\200\235\n\n## 3. Three-second splash screen\n\nImplement a branded splash experience lasting approximately **3 seconds** on a fresh app launch.\n\nShow:\n\n- A simple touchscreen or fingertip icon.\n- \342\200\234Touchscreen Test Tool.\342\200\235\n- Tagline: \342\200\234Check every touch.\342\200\235\n- A subtle loading animation.\n\nImplementation requirements:\n\n- Integrate correctly with Android\342\200\231s system splash behavior.\n- Avoid two consecutive branded splash screens or adding an extra 3-second delay after a separate custom splash.\n- Count the intentional splash duration from the beginning of the app\342\200\231s launch presentation as closely as the platform allows.\n- Do not block the UI thread or use Thread.sleep.\n- Transition automatically to Home.\n- Do not replay the splash when rotating the device, returning from a test, or briefly resuming the app.\n- Explain that system startup or a busy device can make total launch time exceed exactly 3 seconds.\n\n## 4. Touch Coverage Test\n\nCreate a nearly full-screen test canvas with a visible grid.\n\n### Interaction\n\n- Users drag one or more fingers across the screen.\n- Cells change color when an actual touch path passes through them.\n- Show an immediate touch marker and optional trail.\n- Preserve covered cells until Reset or the session ends.\n- Use clear states:\n  - Untested: dark gray.\n  - Touched: green.\n  - Active touch: cyan.\n- Include concise instructions: \342\200\234Drag across every cell. Gray cells have not registered a touch during this test.\342\200\235\n\n### Coverage accuracy\n\n- Define coverage as the percentage of grid cells visited by recorded touch paths.\n- Display touched cells, total cells, and coverage percentage.\n- Map coordinates to the actual drawable test bounds, accounting for insets and layout.\n- Process historical touch samples when available.\n- Interpolate between successive samples from the same active pointer to avoid gaps caused by event spacing.\n- Do not connect separate gestures or different fingers.\n- Clip paths to canvas bounds.\n- Explain in How to Test that interpolation estimates the path between reported samples; a filled cell is not a guarantee that every pixel in it is responsive.\n- Never automatically label an untouched cell \342\200\234dead\342\200\235 or \342\200\234broken.\342\200\235 Use \342\200\234Untested\342\200\235 or \342\200\234Potential issue\342\200\224retest this area.\342\200\235\n\n### Controls\n\nProvide:\n\n- Reset, with confirmation if progress exists.\n- Grid density: coarse, medium, and fine.\n- Show/hide trails.\n- Show/hide grid.\n- Finish Test.\n- Elapsed time and coverage percentage.\n\nWhen grid density changes, clearly explain that coverage will reset and request confirmation if necessary.\n\n### Results\n\nShow:\n\n- Coverage percentage.\n- Touched and untouched cell counts.\n- Test duration.\n- A visual coverage map.\n- Interpretation: \342\200\234Untouched areas may have been missed. Repeat the test carefully before drawing conclusions.\342\200\235\n- Retest and Return Home actions.\n\nDo not declare the hardware healthy solely because coverage reaches 100%.\n\n## 5. Multitouch Test\n\nCreate a full-screen multitouch canvas.\n\nFor each active finger:\n\n- Draw a clearly visible colored circle.\n- Use a stable color based on pointer ID.\n- Label the pointer and display its X/Y coordinates within the test canvas.\n- Optionally draw a short fading trail.\n\nDisplay:\n\n- Current simultaneous touches.\n- Peak simultaneous touches observed during this session.\n- Session duration.\n- A clear Reset Peak action.\n\n### Correct pointer handling\n\nUse real Android MotionEvent data through an appropriate native View/Compose integration or an equally reliable approach.\n\nCorrectly handle:\n\n- ACTION_DOWN.\n- ACTION_POINTER_DOWN.\n- ACTION_MOVE.\n- ACTION_POINTER_UP.\n- ACTION_UP.\n- ACTION_CANCEL.\n- Stable pointer IDs versus changing pointer indices.\n- Multiple fingers lifting or moving independently.\n- Cancellation, app backgrounding, and interruption.\n- Clearing active contacts without erasing the recorded session peak.\n\nNever hardcode a claimed maximum supported touch count.\n\nUse wording such as:\n\n\342\200\234Peak observed: 5 simultaneous touches.\342\200\235\n\nExplain that the peak observed during a session is not proof of the device\342\200\231s absolute hardware limit.\n\nDo not fabricate pressure, touch size, sampling rate, or latency measurements. These are not required for the core app.\n\n## 6. Full-screen behavior and edge testing\n\n- Support an immersive test mode to maximize available test space.\n- Keep the screen awake only while a test is active.\n- Restore normal system UI behavior after leaving a test.\n- Provide a visible, reliable Exit control.\n- Let the control panel collapse so users can test the space beneath it.\n- Exclude areas covered by active controls from coverage calculations or clearly show that they are unavailable.\n- Explain that system gestures, navigation areas, display cutouts, and OS-reserved regions may limit edge testing.\n- Do not claim the app can capture touches that Android does not deliver.\n\nEnsure users can exit even when part of their screen is difficult to use. Preserve normal system Back behavior where permitted by Android.\n\n## 7. Settings\n\nInclude only functional settings:\n\n- Theme: dark, light, or system.\n- Grid density.\n- Touch trail visibility.\n- Coordinate visibility in multitouch mode.\n- Keep screen awake during testing.\n\nSave lightweight preferences locally using DataStore.\n\nDo not add a database unless a real requirement needs one.\n\n## 8. Lifecycle and reliability\n\n- Keep test state in an appropriate ViewModel.\n- Preserve progress across ordinary configuration changes.\n- Clear active pointers when the app loses focus or a gesture is canceled.\n- Pause elapsed active-test time while the app is backgrounded.\n- Avoid accidentally marking cells when users tap test controls.\n- Handle orientation changes with an explicit policy that prevents misleading coverage: either maintain a valid normalized mapping or reset coverage with a clear explanation.\n- Document whether unfinished sessions survive process death. Do not imply persistence that has not been implemented.\n\n## 9. Help and diagnostic limits\n\nProvide a short How to Test screen:\n\n1. Clean and dry the screen.\n2. Start Touch Coverage Test.\n3. Slowly drag across all grid cells.\n4. Repeat around any area that consistently fails to respond.\n5. Open Multitouch Test and place several fingers on the screen.\n6. Observe the current and peak touch counts.\n\nClearly explain:\n\n- Untouched cells may simply have been missed.\n- Screen protectors, moisture, gloves, and system gestures can affect results.\n- The app cannot calibrate, repair, or definitively diagnose touchscreen hardware.\n- No software test can observe a touch event the operating system never reports.\n- Multitouch results depend on the contacts actually detected during the session.\n\nUse cautious, helpful wording without alarming users.\n\n## 10. Performance and accessibility\n\n- Keep touch rendering responsive.\n- Avoid expensive allocations and full-screen recompositions on every event.\n- Bound trail history so memory usage does not grow indefinitely.\n- Use native Canvas drawing where appropriate.\n- Provide content descriptions for standard controls.\n- Do not rely solely on color for instructions or results.\n- Support readable text scaling.\n- Explain any limitations when accessibility services consume or transform touch gestures.\n\n## 11. Verification\n\nInclude focused tests or a concrete manual verification checklist covering:\n\n- The splash appears once and transitions after approximately 3 seconds.\n- Touch coordinates align with the displayed grid.\n- Slow and fast swipes produce sensible coverage.\n- Separate gestures are never connected.\n- Multiple pointers retain stable identities.\n- Lifting one finger does not remove unrelated contacts.\n- Active count returns to zero after release or cancellation.\n- Peak count remains until reset.\n- Reset and grid changes behave correctly.\n- Backgrounding and rotation do not produce false coverage.\n- Test controls do not add coverage.\n- Screen-awake behavior stops after leaving the test.\n- The app runs without a network connection.\n\nDo not claim that a build or test passed unless you actually ran it. Distinguish completed checks from checks requiring a physical Android device.\n\n## 12. Deliverables\n\nProvide:\n\n1. A short implementation overview.\n2. The complete project structure and all required source files.\n3. Exact Android Studio setup and run instructions.\n4. A brief explanation of the touch-event processing and coverage calculation.\n5. Verification results and remaining physical-device checks.\n6. Any limitations stated plainly.\n\nPrioritize accurate touch handling, a usable test canvas, and a polished 3-second splash experience. Implement the complete core app before adding optional features.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
